# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ADJUDICATION LAYER — the reviewed half of a vulnerability pipeline
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Soufflé / CodeQL compute the flows. This file decides what they MEAN.
# The split is not stylistic: nibli must never be handed a relation that
# needs recursion to derive (see README.md § Why the seam is here).
#
# Everything below is meant to be read in a pull request by someone who
# does not write Datalog.

# ─── The trust boundary ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `admits` closes the base vocabulary. The analyzer may state THESE four
# relations and nothing else; a new Soufflé output relation that nobody
# reviewed fails the load instead of quietly widening the record.
# ORDERING IS ENFORCED — this block must precede every ordinary
# assertion, or a late `admits` would grandfather everything above it.
admits("carries").
admits("dangerous").
admits("prevents").
admits("permits").

# `derived_only` is the other half: the verdict is not assertable. The
# analyzer supplies evidence and can never hand us a conclusion.
derived_only("authorized").
derived_only("warns").

# ─── The policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FAIL-CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION. A dangerous flow is authorized only on
# POSITIVE evidence — a sanitizer on the path, or a recorded human
# waiver. There is deliberately no "not known to be dangerous" rule:
# under the closed-world assumption that would auto-clear every sink the
# analyzer failed to classify, which is precisely the bug this layer exists
# to prevent.
#
# `carries(carrier, cargo, destination, origin, route)` — a taint flow.
# `dangerous(danger, victim, conditions)`               — sink classification.
# `prevents(preventer, prevented)`                      — sanitizer on the path.
# `permits(permitter, act, conditions)`                 — recorded review waiver.

all $f, $d, $s, $o, $r: carries($f, $d, $s, $o, $r) & dangerous($s, $d, Exploit) & prevents(Sanitizer, $f) -> authorized($f, Release, $s).
all $f, $d, $s, $o, $r: carries($f, $d, $s, $o, $r) & dangerous($s, $d, Exploit) & permits(Review, $f, Waiver) -> authorized($f, Release, $s).

# ─── Ask for the FINDING, not the clearance ──────────────────────────
#
# `warns(Gate, flow, sink)` — this flow was not cleared, so it is a finding.
#
# This rule is not sugar. The two rules above are PURELY POSITIVE, and a
# purely positive cone that resolves definitively is never materialised
# (GUARANTEES §Completeness) — so `? authorized(...)` returning FALSE is an
# exhaustive backward-chaining search, and it goes quadratic: measured 0.9 s
# at 500 flows and 19.2 s at 2,000. Reading the finding under `~` requests
# the cone, the whole clearance relation is saturated bottom-up, and the same
# question answers in 0.2 s at 2,000 flows and 1.2 s at 10,000.
#
# The fail-closed reading is UNCHANGED and is why this is stated over
# clearance rather than over evidence: a flow whose sink the analyzer never
# classified is not cleared, so it warns.
all $f, $d, $s, $o, $r: carries($f, $d, $s, $o, $r) & ~authorized($f, Release, $s) -> warns(Gate, $f, $s).
